value "3{m}^{5}+4{m}^{4}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407072} :answer-field/id #uuid "e351293d-9061-577e-a773-504fba7cccd5" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407121}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "56052108-5098-50da-941c-873f362e867d" :question/math-academy-id "q-184080" :question/problem "Suppose $n=6{m}^{5}+8{m}^{4}-1,$ where $m\\in Z.$ Complete the following argument." :question/worked-solution "An integer is *odd* if it can be written as $2a+1,$ where $a$ is an integer.\n\nNow, note the following regarding the expression $6{m}^{5}+8{m}^{4}-1:$\n\n- All terms, apart from the constant term, have a common factor of $2.$\n\n- The constant term can be written as $-1=-2+1.$\n\nTherefore, we complete our argument as follows:\n\nThe number $n$ is $\\text{odd}$ since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =6{m}^{5}+8{m}^{4}-1 \\\\  & =(6{m}^{5}+8{m}^{4}-2)+1 \\\\  & =2({{3{m}^{5}+4{m}^{4}-1}_{⏟}}_{a})+1 \\\\  & =2a+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a=3{m}^{5}+4{m}^{4}-1$ is an integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407125 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171931 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba331138-93b8-5fb0-918b-17dc6d3e4029" :knowledge-point/title "Showing a Linear Expression Is Even"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10a6a47a-33d5-5bed-8a8d-7bef18cbabf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5m+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407024} {:answer/id #uuid "686571cc-efdf-579d-9ef1-4ec16b544d68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5m-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407048} {:answer/id #uuid "84b2ca94-8ca7-5027-9aeb-dff194b65b21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5m+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407058}] :ans